--TEST--
`implements` names an interface only, so a class of the `Throwable` tree after it does not compile, and neither does a reserved interface after `extends` in a class — `rule:errors/throwable-hierarchy`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Refused: `Throwable` is a class.
class Lost implements Throwable {}

// Refused: `RuntimeError` is a class.
class Broken implements RuntimeError {}

// Refused: `RuntimeError` is a class, even beside an `extends`.
class Both extends LogicError implements RuntimeError {}

// Refused: `Comparable` is an interface.
class Ordered extends Comparable {}

// Accepted: a class joins the tree with `extends`.
class Missing extends RuntimeError {}
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0303]: `Throwable` is a class, not an interface
%A
error[E0303]: `RuntimeError` is a class, not an interface
%A
error[E0303]: `RuntimeError` is a class, not an interface
%A
error[E0303]: `Comparable` is an interface, not a class
%A
error: aborting due to 4 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
